WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.073
<v Examiner>Case L, question five.

2
00:00:03.073 --> 00:00:04.056
<v Examiner>Otosclerosis.

3
00:00:04.056 --> 00:00:04.887
<v Examiner>Four marks.

4
00:00:05.337 --> 00:00:08.902
<v Examiner>A CT scan of Jack's ears shows no signs of otosclerosis.

5
00:00:08.902 --> 00:00:12.276
<v Examiner>What kind of deafness would otosclerosis have caused?

6
00:00:12.276 --> 00:00:16.350
<v Examiner>What specific structure of the ear is damaged by that condition,

7
00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:21.252
<v Examiner>in what part of the ear is it found, and how does that damage affect hearing?

8
00:00:21.702 --> 00:00:24.075
This is the flip side of Jack's case.

9
00:00:24.075 --> 00:00:25.999
His deafness is sensorineural.

10
00:00:25.999 --> 00:00:28.371
Otosclerosis would be the other kind.

11
00:00:28.371 --> 00:00:32.411
So really, this question is asking: do you know the difference?

12
00:00:32.861 --> 00:00:36.552
The hook: ossicles stuck, no vibration, conduction deafness.

13
00:00:37.002 --> 00:00:38.422
What kind of deafness?

14
00:00:38.422 --> 00:00:39.713
Conduction deafness.

15
00:00:39.713 --> 00:00:43.263
Something stops sound being conducted to the inner ear.

16
00:00:43.263 --> 00:00:47.975
Her deck lists otosclerosis right alongside earwax, a perforated eardrum,

17
00:00:47.975 --> 00:00:49.653
and middle ear infections.

18
00:00:50.103 --> 00:00:51.133
Which structure?

19
00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:56.157
The ossicles: the three tiny ear bones, the malleus, the incus and the stapes.

20
00:00:56.157 --> 00:00:58.991
Her deck calls it sclerosis of the ossicles,

21
00:00:58.991 --> 00:01:02.405
and Patton says the irregularities are in the stapes.

22
00:01:02.405 --> 00:01:06.849
So name the ossicles, and for the extra detail, the stapes above all.

23
00:01:07.299 --> 00:01:08.254
Where are they?

24
00:01:08.254 --> 00:01:09.400
In the middle ear.

25
00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:14.238
Their job is to carry the vibration from the eardrum, the tympanic membrane,

26
00:01:14.238 --> 00:01:15.893
across to the oval window.

27
00:01:15.893 --> 00:01:19.267
The stapes is the one that pushes on the oval window.

28
00:01:19.717 --> 00:01:21.381
How does that affect hearing?

29
00:01:21.381 --> 00:01:25.800
In otosclerosis, the ossicles become attached to the walls of the middle ear.

30
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:30.677
That's her wording, from her ear learning page, and it's worth writing word for word:

31
00:01:30.677 --> 00:01:34.120
the ossicles become attached to the walls of the middle ear,

32
00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.243
which interferes with the vibrations.

33
00:01:36.243 --> 00:01:40.834
Stuck bones can't vibrate, so they can't amplify the sound onto the oval window,

34
00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:43.760
and the sound never properly reaches the inner ear.

35
00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:48.838
And one more line, for the comparison: the inner ear and its hair cells are fine,

36
00:01:48.838 --> 00:01:52.437
so sound can still be conducted through the bones of the skull.

37
00:01:52.437 --> 00:01:55.922
That's the difference from Jack, whose hair cells are broken.

38
00:01:57.372 --> 00:01:57.986
The traps.

39
00:01:57.986 --> 00:02:01.363
Otosclerosis is conduction deafness, not sensorineural.

40
00:02:01.363 --> 00:02:04.802
Her multiple choice key comes at it from the other side:

41
00:02:04.802 --> 00:02:09.592
sensorineural deafness can come from everything except damage to the ossicles.

42
00:02:09.592 --> 00:02:11.802
Second: name the specific structure.

43
00:02:11.802 --> 00:02:14.566
"The middle ear" is a place, not a structure.

44
00:02:14.566 --> 00:02:16.408
The structure is the ossicles.

45
00:02:16.408 --> 00:02:18.311
Third: keep your ears straight.

46
00:02:18.311 --> 00:02:19.601
Ossicles, middle ear.

47
00:02:19.601 --> 00:02:22.119
Organ of Corti and hair cells, inner ear.

48
00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:23.160
<v Examiner>Once more.

49
00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:28.307
<v Examiner>Otosclerosis: what kind of deafness, what's damaged, where, and how it affects hearing.

50
00:02:30.757 --> 00:02:31.954
Conduction deafness.

51
00:02:31.954 --> 00:02:35.364
The ossicles, the malleus, incus and stapes, are damaged.

52
00:02:35.364 --> 00:02:40.330
They sit in the middle ear and carry vibration from the eardrum to the oval window.

53
00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:44.398
In otosclerosis they become attached to the walls of the middle ear,

54
00:02:44.398 --> 00:02:49.423
so they can't vibrate or amplify the sound, and it isn't conducted to the inner ear.

55
00:02:49.423 --> 00:02:51.936
The inner ear and its hair cells are fine,

56
00:02:51.936 --> 00:02:55.227
which is the difference from Jack's sensorineural loss.
